Model answer: B. NAD+ accepts electrons., C. NADH is formed.

Reduction of NAD+ involves acceptance of electrons, commonly together with a hydrogen equivalent, producing NADH. NADH then serves as an electron carrier in catabolic metabolism. ATP is not produced directly by the reduction step, and carbon dioxide formation depends on specific metabolic reactions rather than NAD+ reduction itself. Redox coenzymes link substrate oxidation to cellular energy conservation.

