Model answer: B. 2 ATP (net), C. 2 NADH

Glycolysis converts glucose to pyruvate in the cytoplasm, producing a net gain of two ATP and two NADH molecules per glucose. Carbon dioxide is not released during glycolysis and acetyl-CoA is generated later during pyruvate oxidation. Glycolysis provides both energy and metabolic intermediates, forming the first major stage of glucose catabolism in most cells.

